IMPORT prelude

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (Western Australia) -- what the Act does to a claim for damages for personal injury, from the
-- seat of the injured person.
--
-- SEAT: the injured person claiming damages ("Does the Act cover my claim, or does another Act? What thresholds and caps
-- limit my damages? Will I be held partly to blame? Does an obvious risk, a risk warning or a waiver defeat my claim?").
-- Every exported question is phrased as that person would ask it. What the Act tells a court to do or not do is encoded as
-- what it means for that person: what can be awarded to them, and what defeats their claim.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3 (harm, personal injury, personal injury damages); s 3A and its Table (the classes of damages the
-- Act's Parts do not reach: intentional and sexual harm, motor vehicle (CTP) claims, workers' compensation common-law claims
-- against the employer, tobacco, aviation, asbestos and silica); s 4 (indexing by the WA Wage Price Index, rounded to $500);
-- s 5A (Part 1A, and the 1 December 2003 start); s 5B (when a person is liable for failing to take precautions); ss 5E-5J
-- (recreational activities: dangerous recreational activities and obvious risks, risk warnings, waivers in recreational
-- services contracts); ss 5K, 5L (contributory negligence, and the presumption if I was intoxicated); ss 5N, 5O, 5P (obvious
-- risk: presumed awareness, no duty to warn; inherent risk); s 6 (Part 2's reach); s 9 (general damages: the Amount A
-- threshold and the band up to Amount C); s 10 (Amounts A and C); s 11 (loss of earnings: 3 times average weekly
-- earnings); s 12 (gratuitous home care: Amount B and the weekly and hourly caps); s 13 (Amount B).
--
-- Edition: the Civil Liability Act 2002 (No. 35 of 2002) as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49335.txt
-- (legislation.wa.gov.au mrdoc 49335, retrieved 2026-09-07); its compilation table runs to the Evidence Act 2025 (No. 15 of
-- 2025) among the uncommenced provisions, and its s 3A Table includes the amendment by No. 32 of 2024 (dust diseases). No
-- regulations are used: the indexed amounts are fixed by s 4 and published by the Minister's notice in the Gazette
-- (ss 10(3), 13(3)), which is "for public information only" (ss 10(4), 13(4)). No notice is deposited.
--
-- NUMBERS THAT ARE INPUTS. Amounts A and C (s 10) and Amount B (s 13) for a year after 2002-03 are obtained by indexing the
-- year before by the WA Wage Price Index (s 4); the index figures are the Australian Statistician's and are not deposited,
-- so the year's amounts are inputs. The 2002-03 starting amounts ($12 000, $36 500, $5 000) are in the text and are
-- encoded. The average weekly earnings figures (ss 11(3), 12(5)) are the Australian Statistician's, so they are inputs.
--
-- THE s 9(3) FORMULA was an image missing from the deposited text; it is transcribed by hand from the authorised Word
-- rendering of the same document (mrdoc 49335) in ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49335.formulas.md (2026-10-21):
-- "Amount A - (Amount assessed - Amount C)", and is encoded.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Part 1A Divisions 2 and 3 beyond s 5B (causation, s 5C, and
-- the onus, s 5D, are for the court and are noted only); Division 7 (professional negligence); Part 1B (mental harm);
-- Part 1C (public bodies); Part 1CA (volunteer staff); Part 1D (good samaritans); Part 1E (apologies); Part 1F
-- (proportionate liability, which s 5AI confines to economic loss and property damage, "not including any claim arising out
-- of personal injury"); s 13A (CISS participants: asked of the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act instead); Part 2
-- Division 4 (structured settlements) and Division 5 (provisional damages for dust diseases); Part 2A (child sexual abuse
-- actions); Part 3 (advertising and touting); the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence and Tortfeasors' Contribution) Act 1947,
-- which sets how far damages are reduced for contributory negligence (this Act sets only the standard and the presumption).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the injured person, with the design defaults
-- ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as
-- advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Conventions. Money is in dollars. Periods are in the unit the name says (weeks, hours).

§ `Civil Liability Act 2002 -- the injured person claiming damages`

§§ `Arithmetic`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER
      `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `s 3, s 3A, s 5A, s 6 -- which parts of the Act reach my claim`

-- s 3: "personal injury damages means damages that relate to personal injury to a person caused by the fault of another
-- person, but does not include a sum payable under a superannuation scheme or any life or other insurance policy".
-- "personal injury includes -- (a) death; and (b) prenatal injury; and (c) impairment of a person's physical or mental
-- condition; and (d) disease".
-- s 8: "This Act, other than Part 2A, does not create or confer any cause of civil action". The Act only shapes a claim the
-- general law (negligence, contract) already gives; whether I have one is not encoded.
--
-- s 3A(1) Table: the provisions in the third column "do not apply to damages of a class specified in the second column":
--   item 1 -- "an unlawful intentional act that is done with an intention to cause personal injury" or a sexual offence or
--             unlawful sexual conduct: "Parts 1A, 1C, 1CA, 1D, 1E and 2 (other than section 10A)";
--   item 2 -- "Damages to which the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies": "Parts 1C, 1E and 2 (other than
--             section 10A and Division 4)";
--   item 3 -- damages to which the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023 Part 7 Division 2 applies, and those
--             in its s 416(a): "Parts 1A, 1B, 1C, 1D, 1E and 2 (other than section 10A and Divisions 4 and 5)";
--   item 4 -- "smoking or other use of tobacco products": "Parts 1A (other than sections 5A, 5B, 5C and 5D), 1B, 1D, 1E and
--             2 (other than section 10A and Division 4)";
--   item 5 -- "Damages under the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1961": "Parts 1A, 1B, 1C, 1D, 1E, 1F and 2";
--   item 6 -- "the inhalation of asbestos or silica": as item 4, but keeping Divisions 4 and 5 of Part 2.
-- Whether the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act or Part 7 Division 2 of the workers' compensation Act applies to the
-- damages is a borrowed question, asked as a fact (see the baselines of those Acts).
DECLARE `What caused my injury` IS ONE OF
    `the negligence or other fault of another person`
    `an unlawful act intended to cause personal injury`                     -- item 1(a)
    `a sexual offence or other unlawful sexual conduct`                      -- item 1(b)
    `smoking or other use of tobacco products`                               -- item 4
    `inhaling asbestos or silica`                                            -- item 6
    `carriage by air, claimed under the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1961`   -- item 5

-- s 5A(3): Divisions 2 to 6 of Part 1A apply only if the harm "arises out of an incident happening on or after 1 December
-- 2003". s 6(4): Part 2 "applies only if the personal injury arises out of an incident happening after the commencement of
-- this Part". The compilation table puts the Act (other than ss 1 and 2) in operation on 1 January 2003; that Part 2
-- commenced then is the encoder's reading. ss 5A(4), 6(5): if it "cannot be ascertained" when the incident happened, it is
-- taken to have happened after the start date if "the symptoms of the injury first appeared" after it.
DECLARE `When it happened` IS ONE OF
    `on or after 1 December 2003`
    `from 1 January 2003 to 30 November 2003`
    `before 1 January 2003`
    `not known; symptoms first appeared on or after 1 December 2003`
    `not known; symptoms first appeared from 1 January to 30 November 2003`
    `not known; symptoms first appeared before 1 January 2003`

DECLARE `My claim` HAS
    `what caused my injury`                                                  IS A `What caused my injury`
    `when the incident happened`                                             IS A `When it happened`
    `the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies to the damages`  IS A BOOLEAN   -- item 2
    `it is a claim against my employer under Part 7 Division 2 of the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023`  IS A BOOLEAN   -- item 3
    `what I seek is a superannuation or life or other insurance benefit`     IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 3: not "personal injury damages"

GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the incident is on or after 1 December 2003` IF
    CONSIDER `the claim`'s `when the incident happened`
    WHEN `on or after 1 December 2003` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `not known; symptoms first appeared on or after 1 December 2003` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the incident is after Part 2 commenced` IF
    CONSIDER `the claim`'s `when the incident happened`
    WHEN `before 1 January 2003` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `not known; symptoms first appeared before 1 January 2003` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm was intentional or sexual` IF
       `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `an unlawful act intended to cause personal injury`
    OR `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `a sexual offence or other unlawful sexual conduct`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the harm was from tobacco, asbestos or silica` IF
       `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `smoking or other use of tobacco products`
    OR `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `inhaling asbestos or silica`

DECLARE `Part 1A for me` IS ONE OF
    `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    `only ss 5A-5D: the general duty and causation`
    `none of Part 1A`

-- Part 1A is the Act's set of liability rules: it decides whether the person who hurt me is liable at all, and whether I
-- am partly to blame. s 5A(1): it "applies to any claim for damages for harm caused by the fault of a person", subject to
-- s 3A. It still applies to a CTP (motor vehicle) claim: item 2 does not list Part 1A.
@export Which of the Civil Liability Act's liability rules (Part 1A) apply to my claim (ss 3A, 5A)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury, when it happened, and whether the claim is a motor vehicle (CTP) or workers' compensation claim against your employer
GIVETH A `Part 1A for me`
DECIDE `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `carriage by air, claimed under the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1961`
            THEN `none of Part 1A`
        IF `the harm was intentional or sexual` `the claim`
            THEN `none of Part 1A`
        IF `the claim`'s `it is a claim against my employer under Part 7 Division 2 of the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023`
            THEN `none of Part 1A`
        IF (NOT (`the incident is on or after 1 December 2003` `the claim`))
            THEN `none of Part 1A`
        IF `the harm was from tobacco, asbestos or silica` `the claim`
            THEN `only ss 5A-5D: the general duty and causation`
        OTHERWISE `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`

DECLARE `The limits on my damages` IS ONE OF
    `Part 2 of the Civil Liability Act: the general damages threshold, the earnings cap and the home care limits`
    `the limits in the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943, not Part 2`
    `the limits in Part 7 Division 2 of the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, not Part 2`
    `none of Part 2's limits: the court may only refer to earlier awards (s 10A)`
    `none of Part 2: carriage by air`
    `none of Part 2: the incident was before Part 2 commenced`
    `none: not personal injury damages`

-- s 6(1): "Subject to section 3A, this Part applies to the awarding of personal injury damages". s 7: "A court cannot award
-- damages contrary to Division 2 or 3." Every item of the s 3A Table but item 5 keeps s 10A (earlier awards as a guide), and
-- items 2, 3, 4 and 6 keep Part 2 Division 4 (structured settlements); items 3 and 6 keep Division 5 (dust diseases). None
-- of those is a limit. Where two items could apply (a deliberate act with a car, say), the encoder takes the motor vehicle
-- and workers' compensation items first, since their own Acts then set the limits; either way Part 2's limits are off.
@export Do the Civil Liability Act's limits on damages (Part 2) apply to my claim, or does another Act's (ss 3, 3A, 6)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury, when it happened, and whether the claim is a motor vehicle (CTP) or workers' compensation claim against your employer
GIVETH A `The limits on my damages`
DECIDE `Which limits apply to my damages` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the claim`'s `what I seek is a superannuation or life or other insurance benefit`
            THEN `none: not personal injury damages`
        IF `the claim`'s `what caused my injury` EQUALS `carriage by air, claimed under the Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1961`
            THEN `none of Part 2: carriage by air`
        IF (NOT (`the incident is after Part 2 commenced` `the claim`))
            THEN `none of Part 2: the incident was before Part 2 commenced`
        IF `the claim`'s `the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies to the damages`
            THEN `the limits in the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943, not Part 2`
        IF `the claim`'s `it is a claim against my employer under Part 7 Division 2 of the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023`
            THEN `the limits in Part 7 Division 2 of the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, not Part 2`
        IF `the harm was intentional or sexual` `the claim` OR `the harm was from tobacco, asbestos or silica` `the claim`
            THEN `none of Part 2's limits: the court may only refer to earlier awards (s 10A)`
        OTHERWISE `Part 2 of the Civil Liability Act: the general damages threshold, the earnings cap and the home care limits`

§§ `ss 4, 10, 13 -- the amounts for the year`

-- s 10(1): "For the financial year ending on 30 June 2003 -- (a) Amount A is $12 000; and (b) Amount C is $36 500."
-- s 13(1): "Amount B for the financial year ending on 30 June 2003 is $5 000."
-- ss 10(2), 13(2): for any other year, each is the year before's amount "varied ... according to section 4".
`Amount A for 2002-03` MEANS 12000
`Amount C for 2002-03` MEANS 36500
`Amount B for 2002-03` MEANS 5000

-- s 4(2)(a): vary the preceding year's amount "by the percentage by which" the WA Wage Price Index (ordinary time hourly
-- rates of pay, excluding bonuses) "varied between the last December quarter before the preceding financial year commenced
-- and the last December quarter before the relevant financial year commenced". (3): "If the amount ... is not a multiple of
-- $500 it is to be rounded off to the nearest multiple of $500 (with an amount that is $250 more than a multiple of $500
-- being rounded off to the next highest multiple of $500)." The percentage is the Statistician's; it is an input.
-- (2)(b), the regulations' fallback if the index is not published, is not encoded.
@export What is Amount A, B or C for the next financial year, given this year's amount and the change in the WA Wage Price Index (s 4)?
GIVEN `the amount for the preceding year` IS A NUMBER @desc Amount A, B or C for the preceding financial year, in dollars
      `the WPI change in per cent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the WA Wage Price Index varied between the two December quarters
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `The indexed amount for the year` IS
    (TRUNC ((`the varied amount` PLUS 250) DIVIDED BY 500) 0) TIMES 500
    WHERE
        `the varied amount` MEANS `the amount for the preceding year` TIMES (1 PLUS (`the WPI change in per cent` DIVIDED BY 100))

-- The amounts "for the year in which the amount is assessed" (s 9(1)) and the average weekly earnings "at the date of the
-- award" (s 11(1)) or "for the relevant quarter" (s 12(5)) are inputs. Publication in the Gazette "is for public information
-- only" and an error in it "does not affect what is Amount A or Amount C" (s 10(4)).
DECLARE `The amounts for the year` HAS
    `Amount A`  IS A NUMBER   -- s 10: at or below it, no general damages
    `Amount B`  IS A NUMBER   -- s 13: at or below it, no damages for gratuitous home care
    `Amount C`  IS A NUMBER   -- s 10: above A up to C, only the excess over A
    `average weekly total earnings of full-time adult employees in WA` IS A NUMBER   -- s 11(3)(a)
    `average weekly total earnings of all employees in WA` IS A NUMBER               -- s 12(5)(a)

§§ `s 9 -- general damages (non-pecuniary loss)`

-- s 9(4): "nonpecuniary loss means -- (a) pain and suffering; and (b) loss of amenities of life; and (c) loss of enjoyment
-- of life; and (d) curtailment of expectation of life; and (e) bodily or mental harm."
-- s 9(1): "If the amount of nonpecuniary loss is assessed to be not more than Amount A ... no damages are to be awarded".
-- (2): more than A but not more than C, not "more than the excess of the amount assessed over Amount A".
-- (3): more than C but less than A + C, not more than "the excess of the amount assessed over the amount calculated as
-- follows --", and the amount so calculated is "Amount A - (Amount assessed - Amount C)" (the image, transcribed in
-- mrdoc_49335.formulas.md). The cap therefore rises from (assessed - A) at C to the whole assessment at A + C.
-- At A + C or more, s 9 makes no deduction, and the Act has no maximum: the assessed amount is awarded.
-- The assessment itself is the court's, guided by earlier awards (s 10A); it is an input.
@export How much can I be awarded for pain and suffering and loss of enjoyment of life -- general damages (s 9)?
GIVEN `the amounts` IS A `The amounts for the year` @desc Amounts A and C for the year in which the loss is assessed
      `the assessed loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The court's assessment of your non-pecuniary loss, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `How much general damages can I be awarded` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the assessed loss` AT MOST `the amounts`'s `Amount A` THEN JUST 0
        IF `the assessed loss` AT MOST `the amounts`'s `Amount C` THEN JUST (`the assessed loss` MINUS `the amounts`'s `Amount A`)
        IF `the assessed loss` LESS THAN (`the amounts`'s `Amount A` PLUS `the amounts`'s `Amount C`)
            THEN JUST (`the assessed loss` MINUS
                       (`the amounts`'s `Amount A` MINUS (`the assessed loss` MINUS `the amounts`'s `Amount C`)))
        OTHERWISE JUST `the assessed loss`

@export Is my general damages claim below the threshold, so that I get nothing for pain and suffering (s 9(1))?
GIVEN `the amounts` IS A `The amounts for the year` @desc Amount A for the year in which the loss is assessed
      `the assessed loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The court's assessment of your non-pecuniary loss, in dollars
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my general damages claim below the threshold` IF
    `the assessed loss` AT MOST `the amounts`'s `Amount A`

§§ `s 11 -- loss of earnings`

-- s 11(1): "the court is to disregard earnings lost to the extent that they would have accrued at a rate of more than 3 times
-- the average weekly earnings at the date of the award". (2): past and future loss of earnings and of earning capacity.
-- (3)(a): the average weekly earnings is the Statistician's "average weekly total earnings of fulltime adult employees in
-- Western Australia" for the latest quarter available at the date of the award.
@export What is the most a week of lost earnings that can count towards my damages (s 11)?
GIVEN `the amounts` IS A `The amounts for the year` @desc The average weekly total earnings of full-time adult employees in WA at the date of the award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `The weekly earnings cap` IS
    3 TIMES `the amounts`'s `average weekly total earnings of full-time adult employees in WA`

@export How much of my lost earnings can count towards my damages (s 11)?
GIVEN `the amounts` IS A `The amounts for the year` @desc The average weekly total earnings of full-time adult employees in WA at the date of the award
      `my weekly earnings lost` IS A NUMBER @desc What you would have earned a week but for the injury, in dollars
      `weeks lost` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks of earnings were or will be lost
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much lost earnings can count` IS
    (`the lesser of` `my weekly earnings lost` (`The weekly earnings cap` `the amounts`)) TIMES `weeks lost`

§§ `ss 12, 13 -- gratuitous home care from my family or household`

-- s 12(1): "gratuitous services of a domestic nature or gratuitous services relating to nursing and attendance" given "by a
-- member of the same household or family". (2): nothing "if the services would have been, or would be, provided to the
-- person even if the person had not suffered the personal injury". (3): "If the amount of damages that could, if this
-- subsection did not apply, be awarded under subsection (5) or (7) is Amount B or less, no damages are to be awarded".
-- (5): 40 hours a week or more, not more than the average weekly total earnings of all WA employees, per week; (7): fewer,
-- "an hourly rate that is onefortieth" of that weekly rate. Read here as the value assessed, held to the cap, compared with
-- Amount B (the same reading as the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act baseline gives its s 3D).
@export How much can I be awarded for care my family or household gives me without charge (s 12)?
GIVEN `the amounts` IS A `The amounts for the year` @desc Amount B and the average weekly total earnings of all WA employees for the relevant quarter
      `the care would have been given anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the care would have been given even without the injury
      `hours of care a week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the care is or will be given
      `weeks of care` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many weeks
      `the value assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The court's valuation of the care, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much for gratuitous home care` IS
    IF `the care would have been given anyway` OR `held to the cap` AT MOST `the amounts`'s `Amount B`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `held to the cap`
    WHERE
        `the weekly rate` MEANS `the amounts`'s `average weekly total earnings of all employees in WA`
        `the cap` MEANS
            IF `hours of care a week` AT LEAST 40
            THEN `the weekly rate` TIMES `weeks of care`
            ELSE (`the weekly rate` DIVIDED BY 40) TIMES `hours of care a week` TIMES `weeks of care`
        `held to the cap` MEANS `the lesser of` `the value assessed` `the cap`

§§ `s 5B -- can the person who hurt me be liable for not taking precautions`

-- s 5B(1): "A person is not liable for harm caused by that person's fault in failing to take precautions against a risk of
-- harm unless -- (a) the risk was foreseeable ...; and (b) the risk was not insignificant; and (c) in the circumstances, a
-- reasonable person in the person's position would have taken those precautions." Each limb is the court's finding, an
-- input (s 5B(2) lists what the court weighs). s 5D: I bear the onus of proving causation.
DECLARE `The court's findings on the risk` HAS
    `the risk was foreseeable`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the risk was not insignificant`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `a reasonable person in that position would have taken those precautions`  IS A BOOLEAN

@export Can the person who hurt me be liable for failing to take precautions against the risk (s 5B)?
GIVEN `the findings` IS A `The court's findings on the risk` @desc Whether the court finds the risk foreseeable, not insignificant, and one a reasonable person would have guarded against
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can they be liable for not taking precautions` IF
        `the findings`'s `the risk was foreseeable`
    AND `the findings`'s `the risk was not insignificant`
    AND `the findings`'s `a reasonable person in that position would have taken those precautions`

§§ `ss 5K, 5L -- will I be held partly to blame`

-- s 5K(1): the principles for deciding whether a person is liable "also apply in determining whether the person who suffered
-- harm has been contributorily negligent in failing to take precautions against the risk of that harm". (2): the standard is
-- "that of a reasonable person in the position of that person", on "what that person knew or ought to have known at the
-- time". So the s 5B limbs are applied to me, as the court's findings.
-- s 5L(3): if I was "intoxicated at the time of the act or omission that caused the harm", "it is to be presumed that the
-- person was contributorily negligent unless the plaintiff establishes, on the balance of probabilities, that the person's
-- intoxication did not contribute in any way to the cause of the harm". (2): not if "the court is satisfied that the
-- intoxication was not selfinduced". (4) intoxicated: "affected by alcohol or a drug or other substance ... to such an
-- extent that the person's capacity to exercise reasonable care and skill is impaired".
-- How far the damages are then reduced is for the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence and Tortfeasors' Contribution) Act
-- 1947, not encoded.
DECLARE `My own conduct` HAS
    `the findings on my own precautions`                             IS A `The court's findings on the risk`
    `I was intoxicated when the harm was caused`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court is satisfied the intoxication was not self-induced`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `I prove my intoxication did not contribute in any way`          IS A BOOLEAN

@export Am I presumed to have been partly to blame because I was intoxicated (s 5L)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My own conduct` @desc Whether you were intoxicated, whether it was self-induced, and whether you can prove it did not contribute
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I presumed contributorily negligent` IF
        `me`'s `I was intoxicated when the harm was caused`
    AND (NOT `me`'s `the court is satisfied the intoxication was not self-induced`)
    AND (NOT `me`'s `I prove my intoxication did not contribute in any way`)

@export Will I be held contributorily negligent -- partly to blame for my own injury (ss 5K, 5L)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury, when it happened, and whether the claim is a motor vehicle or workers' compensation claim against your employer
      `me` IS A `My own conduct` @desc The court's findings on your own precautions, and whether you were intoxicated
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will I be held contributorily negligent` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND (   `Am I presumed contributorily negligent` `me`
         OR `Can they be liable for not taking precautions` (`me`'s `the findings on my own precautions`))

§§ `ss 5E-5J -- recreational activities`

-- s 5E: "recreational activity includes -- (a) any sport ...; (b) any pursuit or activity engaged in for enjoyment,
-- relaxation or leisure; and (c) any pursuit or activity engaged in for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure at a place (such
-- as a beach, park or other public open space) where people ordinarily engage in sport or ...". "dangerous recreational
-- activity means a recreational activity that involves a significant risk of harm". s 5F(1): "an obvious risk ... is a
-- risk that, in the circumstances, would have been obvious to a reasonable person in the position of that person";
-- (2)-(4) it may be common knowledge, of low probability, and not physically observable. Whether a risk is "significant"
-- or "obvious" is the court's judgment, an input. s 5G: the Division "applies only in respect of liability for harm
-- resulting from a recreational activity".
DECLARE `What I was doing` IS ONE OF
    `a sport, organised or not`
    `something for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure`
    `work or another activity that was not for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure`

DECLARE `The recreational facts` HAS
    `what I was doing`                                               IS A `What I was doing`
    `the activity involved a significant risk of harm`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `the harm came from something that was an obvious risk of it`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `I had asked them for advice or information about the risk`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `a written law required them to warn me of the risk`             IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `facts` IS A `The recreational facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it was a recreational activity` IF
    NOT (`facts`'s `what I was doing` EQUALS `work or another activity that was not for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure`)

-- s 5H(1): the defendant "is not liable for harm ... suffered ... while the plaintiff engaged in a dangerous recreational
-- activity if the harm is the result of the occurrence of something that is an obvious risk of that activity". (2): "whether
-- or not the plaintiff was aware of the risk". (3): not if "(a) the plaintiff has requested advice or information about the
-- risk from the defendant; or (b) the defendant is required by a written law to warn the plaintiff of the risk".
@export Does the rule on obvious risks of dangerous recreational activities defeat my claim (ss 5E-5H)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `facts` IS A `The recreational facts` @desc What you were doing, and whether the risk was significant and obvious and you asked about it or had to be warned
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the dangerous recreational activity rule defeat my claim` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND `it was a recreational activity` `facts`
    AND `facts`'s `the activity involved a significant risk of harm`
    AND `facts`'s `the harm came from something that was an obvious risk of it`
    AND (NOT `facts`'s `I had asked them for advice or information about the risk`)
    AND (NOT `facts`'s `a written law required them to warn me of the risk`)

-- s 5I(1): no duty of care "in respect of a risk of the activity if the risk was the subject of a risk warning to the
-- plaintiff". (4): a warning "given in a manner that is reasonably likely to result in people being warned of the risk
-- before engaging in the recreational activity"; (5) whether I received or understood it does not matter; (6), (7) oral,
-- written or a sign, and may be general. (8): only a warning "given by or on behalf of the defendant or ... the occupier of
-- the place". Not relied on: (9) the harm "resulted from a contravention of a written law, or a law of the Commonwealth,
-- that establishes specific practices or procedures for the protection of personal safety"; (10) contradicted by the
-- defendant's representation; (11) "the plaintiff was required to engage in the recreational activity by the defendant";
-- (12) "reckless disregard"; (13) "a risk warning to an incompetent person".
-- (16): "child means a person who has reached 16 years but is under 18 years of age"; "incompetent person means a person who
-- is under 18 years of age or who, because of a physical or mental disability, lacks the capacity to understand the risk
-- warning". (2): for a child, a warning to a parent who is not an incompetent person; (3): to another such person who
-- accompanied the child or had the child under control.
DECLARE `Who was warned` IS ONE OF
    `I was`
    `my parent, an adult with capacity`
    `another adult with capacity who was with me or in control of me`
    `nobody`

DECLARE `The warning` HAS
    `who was warned`                                                 IS A `Who was warned`
    `my age in years`                                                IS A NUMBER
    `I lacked the capacity to understand it because of a disability` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was given in a way reasonably likely to warn people before they started`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was given by or for them or the occupier of the place`       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the harm resulted from breach of a personal safety law`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `they told me something about the risk that contradicted it`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `they required me to do the activity`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the harm resulted from their reckless disregard`                IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `w` IS A `The warning`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am an incompetent person under s 5I` IF
    `w`'s `my age in years` LESS THAN 18 OR `w`'s `I lacked the capacity to understand it because of a disability`

GIVEN `w` IS A `The warning`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a child under s 5I` IF
    `w`'s `my age in years` AT LEAST 16 AND `w`'s `my age in years` LESS THAN 18

GIVEN `w` IS A `The warning`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the warning reached someone it can be relied on against` IF
    CONSIDER `w`'s `who was warned`
    WHEN `I was` THEN NOT (`I am an incompetent person under s 5I` `w`)
    WHEN `my parent, an adult with capacity` THEN `I am a child under s 5I` `w`
    WHEN `another adult with capacity who was with me or in control of me` THEN `I am a child under s 5I` `w`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Does a risk warning defeat my claim for an injury in a recreational activity (s 5I)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `facts` IS A `The recreational facts` @desc What you were doing
      `w` IS A `The warning` @desc Who was warned, how and by whom, your age, and what else happened
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does a risk warning defeat my claim` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND `it was a recreational activity` `facts`
    AND `the warning reached someone it can be relied on against` `w`
    AND `w`'s `it was given in a way reasonably likely to warn people before they started`
    AND `w`'s `it was given by or for them or the occupier of the place`
    AND (NOT `w`'s `the harm resulted from breach of a personal safety law`)
    AND (NOT `w`'s `they told me something about the risk that contradicted it`)
    AND (NOT `w`'s `they required me to do the activity`)
    AND (NOT `w`'s `the harm resulted from their reckless disregard`)

-- s 5J(1): "a term of a contract for the supply of recreational services may exclude, restrict or modify any liability to
-- which this Division applies that results from breach of an express or implied warranty that the services will be rendered
-- with reasonable care and skill". (3): a term that the person "engages in any recreational activity concerned at his or her
-- own risk operates to exclude" that liability. Not if the harm resulted from breach of a personal safety law (5) or from
-- "reckless disregard" (6). (4): not for a breach before the section commenced (not encoded).
@export Can a term in my contract for recreational services take away my claim (s 5J)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `facts` IS A `The recreational facts` @desc What you were doing
      `the contract excludes their liability or says I take part at my own risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your contract for the recreational services has a term excluding or limiting liability, or saying you take part at your own risk
      `the harm resulted from breach of a personal safety law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm resulted from breach of a law setting specific safety practices or procedures
      `the harm resulted from their reckless disregard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm resulted from an act or omission in reckless disregard of its consequences
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the contract take away my claim` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND `it was a recreational activity` `facts`
    AND `the contract excludes their liability or says I take part at my own risk`
    AND (NOT `the harm resulted from breach of a personal safety law`)
    AND (NOT `the harm resulted from their reckless disregard`)

§§ `ss 5N, 5O, 5P -- obvious and inherent risks (any activity)`

-- s 5N(1): "the person who suffers harm is presumed to have been aware of the risk of harm if it was an obvious risk, unless
-- the person proves on the balance of probabilities that he or she was not aware of the risk". (2): aware of "the type or
-- kind of risk" is enough.
@export Am I taken to have been aware of the risk that injured me (s 5N)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `it was an obvious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position
      `I prove I was not aware of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove you were not aware even of the type or kind of risk
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I presumed aware of the risk` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND `it was an obvious risk`
    AND (NOT `I prove I was not aware of it`)

-- s 5O(1): "A person (the defendant) does not owe a duty of care to another person (the plaintiff) to warn of an obvious
-- risk to the plaintiff." (2): not if "(a) the plaintiff has requested advice or information about the risk ...; or (b) the
-- defendant is required by a written law to warn ...; or (c) the defendant is a professional and the risk is a risk of harm
-- to the plaintiff from the provision of a professional service". (3): those exceptions do not "give rise to a presumption
-- of a duty to warn" -- so the answer then is only that s 5O does not take the duty away.
DECLARE `A duty to warn me` IS ONE OF
    `no duty to warn me of an obvious risk (s 5O)`
    `s 5O does not take away a duty to warn; the general law decides`

@export Did the person who hurt me have to warn me of the risk (s 5O)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `it was an obvious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position
      `I had asked them about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had asked them for advice or information about the risk
      `a written law required them to warn me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a written law required them to warn you
      `it was a risk of a professional service they gave me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they are a professional and the risk came from their professional service
GIVETH A `A duty to warn me`
DECIDE `Did they have to warn me` IS
    IF      `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
        AND `it was an obvious risk`
        AND (NOT `I had asked them about the risk`)
        AND (NOT `a written law required them to warn me`)
        AND (NOT `it was a risk of a professional service they gave me`)
    THEN `no duty to warn me of an obvious risk (s 5O)`
    ELSE `s 5O does not take away a duty to warn; the general law decides`

-- s 5P(1): the defendant "is not liable for harm ... if the harm is the result of the occurrence of something that cannot be
-- avoided by the exercise of reasonable skill and care by the defendant". (2): it "does not operate to exclude liability in
-- connection with a duty to warn of a risk".
@export Does the inherent-risk rule defeat my claim (s 5P)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My claim` @desc What caused your injury and when, to tell whether Part 1A applies
      `it could not have been avoided by reasonable skill and care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm came from something they could not have avoided with reasonable skill and care
      `my claim is that they failed to warn me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for a failure to warn you of the risk
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the inherent risk rule defeat my claim` IF
        `Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `the claim` EQUALS `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
    AND `it could not have been avoided by reasonable skill and care`
    AND (NOT `my claim is that they failed to warn me`)
